    "body": "Photo: © Danielfela\nBy Charlotte Goldstone\n5 November 2026\nUS importers using ‘delivered duty-paid’ (DDP) shipping arrangements face increasing compliance and financial risks, as Customs and Border Protection (CBP) intensifies its scrutiny of foreign importers of record, according to CargoTrans.\nNunzio De Filippis, co-CEO, and Rennie Alston, director of trade compliance, warned that US buyers could face penalties, shipment seizures, and refused entries, even when the firm is not named as the importer of record.\nDuring CargoTrans’s latest monthly Tariff Tea webinar, the executives said the post-Liberation Day rush towards DDP arrangements was beginning to reverse, as importers reconsidered the risks of handing control of customs formalities to overseas suppliers.\nUnder DDP, sellers assume responsibility for import clearance and associated duties. However, Mr Alston warned: “If you are the US consignee, if you are the US order party, if you are the instigator to the transaction, then you have culpability.”\nCargoTrans said CBP was increasingly investigating whether DDP arrangements were being used to avoid tariffs, particularly where foreign sellers or intermediary platforms acted as non-resident importers of record.\nMr De Filippis said some US buyers had reported shipments being refused entry, seized, or ordered for re-export, including cases involving incorrect importer information or the use of a third-party logistics provider as the ultimate consignee.\nMr Alston warned that the consequences could extend beyond the overseas seller, with US buyers potentially facing penalties linked to their import bonds.\nHe suggested companies should reconsider whether DDP remained appropriate, potentially switching to ‘delivered at place’ (DAP) arrangements, to retain control of the import formalities.\nFor businesses continuing to use DDP, CargoTrans recommended demanding greater transparency from suppliers, including access to customs declarations, transaction documents, and supporting import data.\nMr Alston also highlighted US record-keeping requirements, warning that buyers could not simply claim ignorance of what had been declared on their behalf.\nHe also raised concerns about the growing volume of CBP requests for information, known as CF28s, and urged importers to investigate their records thoroughly before responding.\nMr Alston said companies should avoid rushing to meet the standard 20-day response deadline, without first checking the information internally across their procurement, finance, legal, compliance, and logistics teams. A discrepancy between supplier documentation and the original customs declaration could create additional exposure if an importer inadvertently disclosed a previously unreported compliance problem.\n“Your response has to be measured,” he said.\nThe webinar also highlighted a shift towards CBP issuing notices of action without first requesting additional information through a CF28.\nMr Alston attributed this to greater use of customs data analytics, allowing the agency officials to make decisions using information already available through the Automated Commercial Environment (ACE) system.\nWhere importers disagree with a resulting assessment, they may need to pay the additional duties and subsequently challenge the decision through the ‘protest process’.\nAlongside the enforcement concerns, CargoTrans highlighted several immediate tariff developments.\nRevisions to the US Section 338 tariff coverage for Canadian goods took effect on 30 October, removing 22 tariff classifications from the additional 50% duty, while adding 100 others. And 178 China-related Section 301 product exclusions are scheduled to expire on 9 November, potentially thereafter exposing affected imports to additional duties, unless further action is taken.\nThe company also noted that a proposed extension of the suspension of China-linked vessel fees, until 9 January, had not yet been finalised.\nOn freight markets, CargoTrans expected selective ocean rate softening through November, particularly from North Asia and India to the US east coast. However, South-east Asian capacity remained constrained, it noted.\nFinally, the company warned that rail congestion, labour uncertainty, and the planned 16-month closure of Los Angeles’ Vincent Thomas Bridge could undermine inland delivery reliability.\nIts central message to importers was that cheaper ocean freight offered little benefit if customs exposure and inland disruption increased the overall cost of moving goods.",
